@title IClueCode - Provider Code NFT 
@notice Interface of the ClueCode ERC-721 (Provider Code ownership).
ClueCode stores ownership, grade and canonical form only. It stores no
capacity, zones or partitions (those live in ClueRegistry).

@title ClueCodeControl
@notice Per-code, role-based access control for a Provider Code in the registry.
@dev Follows the ideology of OpenZeppelin's AccessControl, scoped to an individual
     code: roles are identified by `bytes32` and administered per `(code, role)`.
     Control of a code is represented by {CONTROLLER_ROLE}. Vault/custody and status
     concerns live in ClueCodeVault, which accesses this control layer for authorization.

@notice Grants `role` for `code` to `account`.
 @dev Caller must hold the role's admin role for `code`. {CONTROLLER_ROLE} cannot
      be granted publicly; it is managed by the vault lifecycle.

@notice Revokes `role` for `code` from `account`.
 @dev Caller must hold the role's admin role for `code`. {CONTROLLER_ROLE} cannot
      be revoked publicly; it is managed by the vault lifecycle.

@title ClueCodeVault
@notice ClueRegistry component that holds ClueCode Provider Code tokens on behalf of
        their controllers (depositors).
@dev Authorization to a code is accessed through {ClueCodeControl}. A vaulted code can
     be released back to its controller only while it is Stateless, meaning it holds
     no Clue ID and no link. The registry that inherits this vault moves a code between
     Stateless and Active with `_lock`/`_unlock` or `_setStatus`.
     Admin: the address that activates a code is recorded as the code's admin. The
     admin is set once, at activation, and cleared on release.

@notice On-chain status of a Provider Code.
@dev Open is the zero value, so a code that is not vaulted reads as Open.
